WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.526
- Say hi to Taylor.
(audience cheers)

00:00:02.526 --> 00:00:03.359
- The winner.

00:00:03.359 --> 00:00:04.192
The winner.
- I know.

00:00:04.192 --> 00:00:05.025
- The winner.

00:00:05.025 --> 00:00:05.858
The winner.

00:00:05.858 --> 00:00:06.691
The winner of American Idol is.

00:00:06.691 --> 00:00:08.160
- He's literally only said
that to so many people.

00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:09.030
We're two of them.

00:00:09.030 --> 00:00:10.269
That's so cool.

00:00:10.269 --> 00:00:11.102
- Good to see you guys.

00:00:11.102 --> 00:00:14.040
- Is it, I found out something
new that I did not know.

00:00:14.040 --> 00:00:15.600
I didn't know that you were originally,

00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:17.400
they maybe almost had you as a judge.

00:00:17.400 --> 00:00:20.730
- Yeah, originally when the
producers, Nigel and Ken--

00:00:20.730 --> 00:00:21.563
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:00:21.563 --> 00:00:23.340
- Were setting up the show.

00:00:23.340 --> 00:00:25.650
They had heard me on the
radio here in Los Angeles

00:00:25.650 --> 00:00:29.460
and they thought that I might
fit the panel to be up there

00:00:29.460 --> 00:00:31.290
with whoever else was already on.

00:00:31.290 --> 00:00:32.490
I guess Simon was already on board

00:00:32.490 --> 00:00:33.750
and I don't know who else at that point.

00:00:33.750 --> 00:00:35.647
And I was like, "Well wait a minute.

00:00:35.647 --> 00:00:37.890
"I would love to audition to be the host."

00:00:37.890 --> 00:00:40.020
And so I auditioned
eventually to be the host

00:00:40.020 --> 00:00:41.220
and I got the job.

00:00:41.220 --> 00:00:42.570
So I'm happy to still have the job.

00:00:42.570 --> 00:00:43.680
- I know, that's incredible.

00:00:43.680 --> 00:00:45.694
I just, I was like, what would it be like

00:00:45.694 --> 00:00:48.160
if you initially were one of the judges?

00:00:48.160 --> 00:00:49.080
- See, the problem is I'm like

00:00:49.080 --> 00:00:50.820
in auto hosts mode all the time.

00:00:50.820 --> 00:00:53.280
So I don't know that I'd be
able to sit behind the desk

00:00:53.280 --> 00:00:54.960
and just give feedback,
I'd want to do that.

00:00:54.960 --> 00:00:56.315
Then toss the commercial,
welcome back, how are you?

00:00:56.315 --> 00:00:57.808
You know, all the--

00:00:57.808 --> 00:00:59.043
(Kelly and audience laughing)

00:00:59.043 --> 00:00:59.876
All, and that would be too much.

00:00:59.876 --> 00:01:01.410
- Are you like this in life,

00:01:01.410 --> 00:01:02.670
like when you're with family--
- Kelly Clarkson.

00:01:02.670 --> 00:01:03.750
- Or like on a date you're just like,

00:01:03.750 --> 00:01:05.270
wait, I'm sorry, I'm trying not--

00:01:05.270 --> 00:01:07.548
- On a date, good evening, how are you?

00:01:07.548 --> 00:01:10.860
We would like to have
the nachos followed by,

00:01:10.860 --> 00:01:11.693
I think, would you like the fajitas?

00:01:11.693 --> 00:01:14.040
Matter of fact, let's have
the beef and chicken fajitas

00:01:14.040 --> 00:01:16.024
and we'll be right back
after we use the bathroom.

00:01:16.024 --> 00:01:16.857
(Kelly and audience laughing)

00:01:16.857 --> 00:01:18.180
- No, so I have a question.

00:01:18.180 --> 00:01:19.350
So did you always know

00:01:19.350 --> 00:01:22.860
'cause it's such a, it's a job

00:01:22.860 --> 00:01:25.200
where I think a few have
really excelled as much as you

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:26.033
and like Dick Clark.

00:01:26.033 --> 00:01:27.258
Like there's--

00:01:27.258 --> 00:01:28.091
- [Ryan] Thank you.

00:01:28.091 --> 00:01:29.160
There's few that have gotten that status

00:01:29.160 --> 00:01:29.993
to where it's just like,

00:01:29.993 --> 00:01:32.580
"Oh my god, that's like such
a pop culture icon, right?"

00:01:32.580 --> 00:01:33.976
So there's very few.
- Thank you.

00:01:33.976 --> 00:01:35.335
- So that's job well done.
(audience cheers)

00:01:35.335 --> 00:01:38.580
But also like, what
were you like as a kid?

00:01:38.580 --> 00:01:40.350
Did you always wanna
do something like this?

00:01:40.350 --> 00:01:44.010
- So as a kid, I wanted to
be on the TV show "CHiPs"

00:01:44.010 --> 00:01:48.000
as a California highway
patrol, biker policeman.

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:48.840
- The stache.

00:01:48.840 --> 00:01:50.580
- Yeah, I wanted to be, you know

00:01:50.580 --> 00:01:52.980
Poncherello or Jon Baker, one
of those guys on the bike.

00:01:52.980 --> 00:01:56.790
So I pretended to do that in
the neighborhood on my Schwinn.

00:01:56.790 --> 00:01:59.515
And I got the, I got from
service merchandise, a siren

00:01:59.515 --> 00:02:03.240
and I pretended I was a CHP officer.

00:02:03.240 --> 00:02:05.040
Well, then I realized, not for me.

00:02:05.040 --> 00:02:09.090
So I also bought a Radio
Shack mixer when I was a kid

00:02:09.090 --> 00:02:10.830
and I pretended to be Casey Kasem,

00:02:10.830 --> 00:02:11.730
I'm counting down the hits

00:02:11.730 --> 00:02:14.158
and pretending to be a deejay.
- [Kelly] Oh my God, I love.

00:02:14.158 --> 00:02:15.516
Yeah, yeah.

00:02:15.516 --> 00:02:18.784
- And when I sort of really got
into recording on cassettes,

00:02:18.784 --> 00:02:21.450
I offered to do outgoing messages

00:02:21.450 --> 00:02:23.490
for answering machines at the time.

00:02:23.490 --> 00:02:24.337
So I would say to my mom,

00:02:24.337 --> 00:02:25.657
"Should I, can I do the outgoing message

00:02:25.657 --> 00:02:27.570
"for the Seacrest family
on the little micro tape?"

00:02:27.570 --> 00:02:28.403
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:02:28.403 --> 00:02:29.236
- Or to the neighbors.

00:02:29.236 --> 00:02:31.410
Listen, I'd love to do
the outgoing message

00:02:31.410 --> 00:02:32.917
for the Tuckers down the street.

00:02:32.917 --> 00:02:34.237
"You've reached the Tucker residence.

00:02:34.237 --> 00:02:35.167
"They're not home right now,

00:02:35.167 --> 00:02:37.254
"but after the beep you
can leave a message."

00:02:37.254 --> 00:02:39.300
And I would try and charge them for that.

00:02:39.300 --> 00:02:40.753
- What an interesting kid.
(audience laughs)

00:02:40.753 --> 00:02:42.535
- [Ryan] I was a little different.

00:02:42.535 --> 00:02:44.880
- It worked out, it worked out.

00:02:44.880 --> 00:02:46.680
So how is Idol different now

00:02:46.680 --> 00:02:48.060
than when Taylor and I were there?

00:02:48.060 --> 00:02:49.290
Because it's different, obviously.

00:02:49.290 --> 00:02:51.780
- Yeah, it's, every, the
world has evolved, right?

00:02:51.780 --> 00:02:52.613
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:02:52.613 --> 00:02:55.642
- I mean music has evolved
in such a great way as well.

00:02:55.642 --> 00:02:57.142
And there are--

00:02:57.142 --> 00:02:58.650
- And these artists have
seen success from it.

00:02:58.650 --> 00:02:59.610
- Well, they've seen the map.

00:02:59.610 --> 00:03:01.410
So you know, they've seen you,

00:03:01.410 --> 00:03:03.210
they've seen Carrie, they've seen Taylor,

00:03:03.210 --> 00:03:06.060
they've seen success
stories, Jennifer as well.

00:03:06.060 --> 00:03:09.180
And so they know that they
can really be a big star.

00:03:09.180 --> 00:03:10.473
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:03:10.473 --> 00:03:11.306
- So now when they're
eight, nine years old,

00:03:11.306 --> 00:03:13.800
they tell me they start
training for the audition.

00:03:13.800 --> 00:03:15.851
So when they're of age to audition,

00:03:15.851 --> 00:03:16.684
they've rehearsed.
- Oh my God, how old are we?

00:03:16.684 --> 00:03:21.210
- Well we, I can tell
you if you wanna know,

00:03:21.210 --> 00:03:23.430
I'm reminded every day I try and jump,

00:03:23.430 --> 00:03:25.350
right here, my right knee.

00:03:25.350 --> 00:03:27.510
But they really practice

00:03:27.510 --> 00:03:28.890
and it's almost like a sport, right?

00:03:28.890 --> 00:03:30.033
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:03:30.033 --> 00:03:31.282
- Like they train for the sport

00:03:31.282 --> 00:03:32.115
of getting to audition for Idol.

00:03:32.115 --> 00:03:33.128
- [Kelly] Wow.

00:03:33.128 --> 00:03:34.320
- And when they get there,
they've got it down.

00:03:34.320 --> 00:03:35.250
They're impressive.

00:03:35.250 --> 00:03:38.310
And the judges, who are really fantastic,

00:03:38.310 --> 00:03:39.183
you know all of them.

00:03:39.183 --> 00:03:40.016
- Yeah.

00:03:40.016 --> 00:03:40.849
- They're so super fantastic.

00:03:40.849 --> 00:03:41.682
They respect each other.

00:03:41.682 --> 00:03:42.515
They love the show.

00:03:42.515 --> 00:03:43.440
- They're so different too.

00:03:43.440 --> 00:03:44.700
I love how that works.

00:03:44.700 --> 00:03:47.640
- They really want everybody to succeed

00:03:47.640 --> 00:03:49.050
and so they work with them.

00:03:49.050 --> 00:03:50.760
You don't get to see all
this because the shows,

00:03:50.760 --> 00:03:51.900
the audition shows are edited,

00:03:51.900 --> 00:03:53.640
but they really work with them to try

00:03:53.640 --> 00:03:55.200
and get them to be their best.

00:03:55.200 --> 00:03:56.033
- Yeah.

00:03:56.033 --> 00:03:58.110
- And you see that and
you see their love for it.

00:03:58.110 --> 00:03:58.943
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:03:58.943 --> 00:03:59.850
- I think that's what makes it magical.

00:03:59.850 --> 00:04:01.560
- Yeah, they're very emotional too.

00:04:01.560 --> 00:04:02.623
I think that's cool as artists

00:04:02.623 --> 00:04:04.380
when they're judges sitting there,

00:04:04.380 --> 00:04:06.180
but they're also those
artists and they connect.

00:04:06.180 --> 00:04:07.030
It's nice to see that.
- It is,

00:04:07.030 --> 00:04:08.130
yeah.
- Yeah,

00:04:08.130 --> 00:04:09.150
'cause it feels authentic

00:04:09.150 --> 00:04:11.451
and that's why people have loved
this show so long, I think.

00:04:11.451 --> 00:04:14.223
We are back with the
host of American Idol,

00:04:14.223 --> 00:04:16.863
Ryan Seacrest, everybody.
(audience cheers)

00:04:16.863 --> 00:04:18.750
- Also sitting in with
the band all hour,

00:04:18.750 --> 00:04:20.580
American Idol winner, Taylor Hicks,

00:04:20.580 --> 00:04:21.510
give it up for him y'all.

00:04:21.510 --> 00:04:22.343
- Thank you.

00:04:23.970 --> 00:04:27.300
So to celebrate Idol turning 21

00:04:27.300 --> 00:04:29.220
you posted this photo
of your 21st birthday.

00:04:29.220 --> 00:04:31.211
Let's check it out, so.
- [Ryan] Oh God.

00:04:31.211 --> 00:04:32.663
(audience applauds)

00:04:32.663 --> 00:04:35.021
Look at those highlights.

00:04:35.021 --> 00:04:35.940
(Kelly laughs)

00:04:35.940 --> 00:04:37.560
- Those are not natural highlights.

00:04:37.560 --> 00:04:39.120
- Those are great sun
(indistinct) highlights.

00:04:39.120 --> 00:04:40.710
- So I don't know if you know
how this works anybody here

00:04:40.710 --> 00:04:42.180
but you go and you sit with foil.

00:04:42.180 --> 00:04:43.260
- [Kelly] Foil.

00:04:43.260 --> 00:04:45.448
- And then they put you into
this big helmet and you--

00:04:45.448 --> 00:04:46.281
- I don't know what you're talking about,

00:04:46.281 --> 00:04:47.114
this is all natural.

00:04:47.114 --> 00:04:48.690
- I don't know if anybody knows this.

00:04:48.690 --> 00:04:50.010
But that was, yeah, that was me.

00:04:50.010 --> 00:04:52.710
And that, what I see in that is my mother

00:04:52.710 --> 00:04:56.370
and also my father leaning
over and the birthday cake.

00:04:56.370 --> 00:04:58.920
I know what that is, that
is a graham cracker crust--

00:04:58.920 --> 00:04:59.753
- [Kelly] Oh.

00:04:59.753 --> 00:05:03.660
- Chocolate fudge bottom with
mint chocolate chip ice cream

00:05:03.660 --> 00:05:05.670
in a cookie pan, in that pan that--

00:05:05.670 --> 00:05:07.440
- Is this your birthday thing
that you have every year?

00:05:07.440 --> 00:05:09.900
- It is, I love it so much.

00:05:09.900 --> 00:05:10.733
I love it so much.

00:05:10.733 --> 00:05:11.720
I'm not like--

00:05:11.720 --> 00:05:12.553
- Who came up with that?

00:05:12.553 --> 00:05:13.770
- My mother, she,

00:05:13.770 --> 00:05:15.125
I dunno if she got it--
- Wow, that's delicious.

00:05:15.125 --> 00:05:16.006
- From like a Julia Child
recipe or something from--

00:05:16.006 --> 00:05:17.280
- I don't know,

00:05:17.280 --> 00:05:18.210
but I love that your birthday thing

00:05:18.210 --> 00:05:19.290
was in one of those Pyrex things.

00:05:19.290 --> 00:05:21.060
It's so me, my childhood.
- It's how we grew up.

00:05:21.060 --> 00:05:22.980
I mean I would eat nachos
out of that same pan.

00:05:22.980 --> 00:05:23.813
- [Kelly] Yeah, me too.

00:05:23.813 --> 00:05:24.713
- The next day.

00:05:24.713 --> 00:05:25.546
- Pull them out of the oven, yeah.

00:05:25.546 --> 00:05:27.120
- But that pan got, I
wish I had that pan now.

00:05:27.120 --> 00:05:27.953
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:05:27.953 --> 00:05:28.791
- If that pan could talk.
- [Kelly] I have plenty.

00:05:28.791 --> 00:05:29.749
(Kelly chuckles)

00:05:29.749 --> 00:05:32.190
- Yeah, so you sometimes
talk about your mom

00:05:32.190 --> 00:05:34.650
on your radio show, does
she like this or not?

00:05:34.650 --> 00:05:36.807
I always ask comics about this too, like--

00:05:36.807 --> 00:05:37.748
- And what do they tell you?

00:05:37.748 --> 00:05:40.290
- It's all over, it's across the board.

00:05:40.290 --> 00:05:41.197
- [Ryan] My mother--

00:05:41.197 --> 00:05:42.030
- They like to be talked about

00:05:42.030 --> 00:05:42.990
but they don't like to be talked about.

00:05:42.990 --> 00:05:45.270
- Well, and which is a very
interesting thing to navigate.

00:05:45.270 --> 00:05:47.640
So my mother and my dad,
they've been married forever.

00:05:47.640 --> 00:05:48.690
They're wonderful--

00:05:48.690 --> 00:05:49.523
- It's something crazy.

00:05:49.523 --> 00:05:50.839
Like over 50 years.

00:05:50.839 --> 00:05:51.672
- [Ryan] Over 50 years,
we're a very close family.

00:05:51.672 --> 00:05:52.896
- Yeah, over 50 years.

00:05:52.896 --> 00:05:53.936
- [Ryan] There they are.

00:05:53.936 --> 00:05:55.151
- That doesn't even happen anymore.

00:05:55.151 --> 00:05:56.220
- I know.

00:05:56.220 --> 00:05:57.390
Don't even get me started.

00:05:57.390 --> 00:05:58.223
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:05:58.223 --> 00:06:00.570
- So they are wonderful

00:06:00.570 --> 00:06:01.800
and they watch and listen to everything.

00:06:01.800 --> 00:06:02.790
Well my mother,

00:06:02.790 --> 00:06:05.670
I will tell a story about
them on a romantic vacation

00:06:05.670 --> 00:06:06.975
or something like this and she says,

00:06:06.975 --> 00:06:08.310
and she likes to do the,

00:06:08.310 --> 00:06:09.517
when she speaks to me, she's like,

00:06:09.517 --> 00:06:11.587
"Ryan John, Ryan John,

00:06:11.587 --> 00:06:13.253
"you don't let all of
our secrets out, do you?"

00:06:13.253 --> 00:06:14.827
I say, "Well mother, I
mean you you're going

00:06:14.827 --> 00:06:15.927
"on an anniversary trip, you know?"

00:06:15.927 --> 00:06:17.407
And she says, "Well, don't, Ryan John

00:06:17.407 --> 00:06:19.237
"don't say anything before we go.

00:06:19.237 --> 00:06:21.428
"I don't want my fans to find me."

00:06:21.428 --> 00:06:23.762
- Oh my God, I love your mum.
- Now mother, do you think

00:06:23.762 --> 00:06:27.480
that they're gonna get on
a Delta flight from Atlanta

00:06:27.480 --> 00:06:29.850
and come find you on this
island in the Caribbean?

00:06:29.850 --> 00:06:31.022
Because they've heard me talk about it?

00:06:31.022 --> 00:06:32.493
- She might end up a "Dateline Special."

00:06:32.493 --> 00:06:33.660
(Ryan laughs)

00:06:33.660 --> 00:06:34.493
If you're that hardcore.

00:06:34.493 --> 00:06:37.017
- But it's cute because
she really enjoys it

00:06:37.017 --> 00:06:38.175
and she really is, she's wonderful.

00:06:38.175 --> 00:06:40.890
And she takes pictures
with people and loves,

00:06:40.890 --> 00:06:42.960
I guess they do recognize
her 'cause she was on--

00:06:42.960 --> 00:06:43.793
- Yeah.

00:06:43.793 --> 00:06:44.626
- "LIVE with Kelly and Ryan"
every now and then too.

00:06:44.626 --> 00:06:45.660
- Yeah, no, I think.

00:06:45.660 --> 00:06:46.493
- It's great to have that.

00:06:46.493 --> 00:06:47.718
I love that relationship with my parents.

00:06:47.718 --> 00:06:48.754
- Yeah.

00:06:48.754 --> 00:06:49.587
- That's great.

00:06:49.587 --> 00:06:50.880
- I love that you have just like,

00:06:50.880 --> 00:06:52.770
such a great support
system, that's amazing.

00:06:52.770 --> 00:06:55.620
Like over 50 years just
having that stability

00:06:55.620 --> 00:06:56.453
in a family--

00:06:56.453 --> 00:06:57.286
- And yet I'm unmarried.

00:06:57.286 --> 00:06:58.119
So I'm trying to figure--

00:06:58.119 --> 00:06:58.952
- You know why though, you know,

00:06:58.952 --> 00:06:59.820
hey, this was a question though.

00:06:59.820 --> 00:07:00.653
You know why though?

00:07:00.653 --> 00:07:03.180
I think you and I are similar
in the fact that I really do,

00:07:03.180 --> 00:07:05.550
like from a young age, I've
always had lots of jobs.

00:07:05.550 --> 00:07:07.927
I often wonder 'cause people ask me this,

00:07:07.927 --> 00:07:09.210
"Oh, do you regret being so busy?"

00:07:09.210 --> 00:07:10.740
And I'm like, I don't feel,

00:07:10.740 --> 00:07:13.410
I think what other people
perceive as really busy.

00:07:13.410 --> 00:07:15.360
I don't perceive as really busy.

00:07:15.360 --> 00:07:16.710
Like I can get exhausted, you know?

00:07:16.710 --> 00:07:19.083
And I do feel like I
could cut back, but like,

00:07:19.083 --> 00:07:20.910
have you kind of always been nine-o?

00:07:20.910 --> 00:07:22.587
Like do you regret being
so busy all the time?

00:07:22.587 --> 00:07:23.970
- No, I have no regrets about it.

00:07:23.970 --> 00:07:26.100
I love, I operate busy.

00:07:26.100 --> 00:07:26.933
Like I do--

00:07:26.933 --> 00:07:27.873
- [Kelly] You too.

00:07:27.873 --> 00:07:28.706
- I do more things when I'm busy.

00:07:28.706 --> 00:07:30.150
If I have one thing to do on a weekend,

00:07:30.150 --> 00:07:31.530
I almost don't get it done.

00:07:31.530 --> 00:07:32.610
So I operate like that.
- Yeah.

00:07:32.610 --> 00:07:35.520
- And I knew, I just knew
what I wanted to do so badly

00:07:35.520 --> 00:07:37.980
and I was lucky enough to
start chipping away at that.

00:07:37.980 --> 00:07:40.350
And so the more I was doing what I loved,

00:07:40.350 --> 00:07:41.940
the more I took on,

00:07:41.940 --> 00:07:44.640
hoping that I could do
it for a longer period,

00:07:44.640 --> 00:07:45.510
long period of time, right?

00:07:45.510 --> 00:07:46.470
Have longevity in it,

00:07:46.470 --> 00:07:47.520
so I don't regret that.
- No one has the kind

00:07:47.520 --> 00:07:48.630
of longevity that you have.

00:07:48.630 --> 00:07:49.800
Like that's incredible.

00:07:49.800 --> 00:07:50.633
- Thank you.

00:07:50.633 --> 00:07:53.100
I could, 30 more years,
I'd love to do this.

00:07:53.100 --> 00:07:54.056
- [Kelly] Yeah, you will be--
- But I--

00:07:54.056 --> 00:07:55.467
- That's, it's crazy.

00:07:55.467 --> 00:07:56.424
- But I--

00:07:56.424 --> 00:07:58.500
(audience applauds)

00:07:58.500 --> 00:07:59.880
It's gone by fast.

00:07:59.880 --> 00:08:02.820
But I've gotten to a place in
my life where I don't regret.

00:08:02.820 --> 00:08:04.050
You know, like I don't, there's

00:08:04.050 --> 00:08:05.200
nothing good about that.
- Me neither.

00:08:05.200 --> 00:08:06.033
Yeah.

00:08:06.033 --> 00:08:06.866
- There's no

00:08:06.866 --> 00:08:07.699
productivity in that.
- No one wins there.

00:08:07.699 --> 00:08:08.532
- No one, no,

00:08:08.532 --> 00:08:09.570
you just stay up all night
thinking about it, right.

00:08:09.570 --> 00:08:10.403
- [Kelly] Yeah.
- It's not worth it.

00:08:10.403 --> 00:08:12.008
- Well, later we have a woman

00:08:12.008 --> 00:08:15.450
who is part of your Seacrest
Studios which I've actually--

00:08:15.450 --> 00:08:16.435
- You've been to some of them.
- I've been to, yeah.

00:08:16.435 --> 00:08:18.450
So can you tell everybody about that?

00:08:18.450 --> 00:08:20.910
- So, years ago I wanted to do something

00:08:20.910 --> 00:08:22.950
for children's hospitals
and I talked to parents

00:08:22.950 --> 00:08:25.530
and patients about their
stays in the hospitals.

00:08:25.530 --> 00:08:29.130
And there was, in the places
I were, there was not,

00:08:29.130 --> 00:08:32.490
the heartbeat a place for, you know,

00:08:32.490 --> 00:08:34.020
people that were in the hospital.

00:08:34.020 --> 00:08:36.094
Both parents and the
patients like the center,

00:08:36.094 --> 00:08:39.090
the nucleus, and a place to escape.

00:08:39.090 --> 00:08:42.540
And so I wanted to create that space

00:08:42.540 --> 00:08:44.340
and I came up with this
idea of media centers,

00:08:44.340 --> 00:08:47.400
radio and TV studios in
the center of the hospital.

00:08:47.400 --> 00:08:48.233
- It's so cool.

00:08:48.233 --> 00:08:49.483
- So that parents could come,

00:08:49.483 --> 00:08:51.300
kids could come down from their beds.

00:08:51.300 --> 00:08:52.440
If they can't get out of their beds,

00:08:52.440 --> 00:08:53.970
they can watch the shows that happen

00:08:53.970 --> 00:08:55.920
and the patients create the programming.

00:08:55.920 --> 00:08:59.160
They produce the shows,
stars like Kelly Clarkson

00:08:59.160 --> 00:09:01.631
and Justin Bieber and
whoever's on tour come by

00:09:01.631 --> 00:09:06.450
and the patients interview the
stars and do their own shows.

00:09:06.450 --> 00:09:07.283
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:09:07.283 --> 00:09:08.549
- And we started with one and
I thought, okay, you know,

00:09:08.549 --> 00:09:10.620
this is really great
in creating some energy

00:09:10.620 --> 00:09:11.620
inside the walls of the hospital.

00:09:11.620 --> 00:09:14.160
Well, now we are gonna have
14 by the end of Summer

00:09:14.160 --> 00:09:15.125
across the country.

00:09:15.125 --> 00:09:15.958
- It's so crazy.
(audience applauds)

00:09:15.958 --> 00:09:18.504
- And my mom and my dad and
my sister all operate it.

00:09:18.504 --> 00:09:20.881
And it really truly, as you've seen,

00:09:20.881 --> 00:09:23.336
it's just a magical place where people,

00:09:23.336 --> 00:09:25.122
patients forget why they're there.

00:09:25.122 --> 00:09:25.955
And we've had--

00:09:25.955 --> 00:09:27.240
- The escapism is nice.

00:09:27.240 --> 00:09:28.597
- We've had some parents say,

00:09:28.597 --> 00:09:31.927
"Ryan, I have to tell you my
child would come for treatment

00:09:31.927 --> 00:09:33.682
"and fight having to go to the hospital.

00:09:33.682 --> 00:09:36.517
"Now my child asks me,
'When do we get treatment?

00:09:36.517 --> 00:09:38.159
"'I wanna go to the Seacrest Studio.'"

00:09:38.159 --> 00:09:39.096
- Oh.

00:09:39.096 --> 00:09:39.953
- And so it's really--

00:09:39.953 --> 00:09:40.786
- Look what you did there.

00:09:40.786 --> 00:09:41.778
- It's changed--

00:09:41.778 --> 00:09:43.020
- To create something in usually

00:09:43.020 --> 00:09:44.447
a negative environment.
- Hopefully, the perception,

00:09:44.447 --> 00:09:45.352
exactly.

00:09:45.352 --> 00:09:46.185
- And a sad environment

00:09:46.185 --> 00:09:47.576
and just an intense one to release.

00:09:47.576 --> 00:09:48.409
- It's changed the feeling a little bit.

00:09:48.409 --> 00:09:49.242
- Yeah.

00:09:49.242 --> 00:09:50.075
- That's what we're doing.

00:09:50.075 --> 00:09:52.323
- Yeah, I think it's the
most beautiful thing.

